window.SLIDE_TEXT["cad-part-4"] = {"1":"<ul><li>Coronary Artery Disease Part IV:</li><li>Acute Coronary Syndrome Management, Complications, and Secondary Prevention</li><li>PHAS 517</li><li>Jessica Stager, MPAS, PA-C</li><li>November 19, 2026</li></ul>",
"2":"<ul><li>Acute Coronary Syndromes</li><li>Despite optimal medical treatment, a patient with CAD may have an acute coronary syndrome event at any time.</li><li>Patients also frequently stop or are non-compliant with medical therapy- increasing their risk of an ACS event.</li></ul>",
"3":"<ul><li>ACS Definitions</li><li>Myocardial Ischemia<ul><li>Inadequate oxygen supply relative to myocardial demand</li><li>May produce ST depression, T-wave inversion, or other ischemic changes.</li></ul></li><li>Myocardial Injury<ul><li>Myocardial damage associated with elevated cardiac troponin.</li></ul></li><li>Myocardial Infarction<ul><li>Irreversible injury to the myocardium</li><li>Requires evidence of acute myocardial injury AND acute myocardial ischemia</li><li>Suggested by EKG changes: ST elevation,  Q waves</li></ul></li></ul>",
"4":"<ul><li>Understanding EKG Changes of Myocardial Ischemia</li></ul>",
"5":"",
"6":"<ul><li>Types of Myocardial Infarction</li><li>Fifth Universal Definition of Myocardial Infarction (published in 2026)</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>New classification</td><td>Description</td><td>Previously associated category</td></tr><tr><td>Primary MI</td><td>Spontaneous acute coronary pathology (e.g., plaque disruption, coronary dissection, vasospasm, or embolism).</td><td>Primarily Type 1; some events previously classified as Type 2 or Type 4b/4c</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary MI</td><td>Myocardial oxygen supply–demand imbalance due to another acute condition (e.g., severe anemia, hypoxemia, or tachyarrhythmia).</td><td>Type 2</td></tr><tr><td>Procedure-related MI</td><td>MI occurring as a complication of a percutaneous or surgical cardiac procedure.</td><td>Types 4a, 4b, 4c, and 5</td></tr></table>",
"7":"<ul><li>Types of Myocardial Infarction</li><li>Fourth Universal Definition (2018)</li></ul>",
"8":"<ul><li>Myocardial Injury vs. Myocardial Infarction</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Clinical scenario</td><td>Troponin</td><td>Evidence of ischemia</td><td>Diagnosis</td></tr><tr><td>Sepsis with elevated troponin, no ischemic symptoms or findings</td><td>Elevated, rising/falling</td><td>No</td><td>Acute myocardial injury</td></tr><tr><td>Sepsis with hypotension, chest pressure, dynamic ST depression and rising troponin</td><td>Elevated, rising/falling</td><td>Yes</td><td>Secondary MI (traditionally Type 2 MI)</td></tr><tr><td>Patient with plaque rupture and acute coronary thrombosis, ST elevation</td><td>Elevated, rising/falling</td><td>Yes</td><td>Primary MI (traditionally Type 1 MI)</td></tr></table>",
"9":"<ul><li>Acute Coronary Syndromes</li><li>Unstable Angina (UA): Due to subtotal coronary artery occlusion<ul><li>Clinically characterized as:</li><li>Chest pain occurring at rest, without</li><li>ST segment elevation- can see EKG changes of ST depression or T wave inversion, and</li><li>NEGATIVE HS troponin</li></ul></li></ul>",
"10":"<ul><li>Acute Coronary Syndromes</li><li>Non-ST segment Elevation Myocardial Infarction (NSTEMI):<ul><li>Subendocardial infarction characterized by chest pain occurring at rest, with</li><li>POSITIVE HS troponin</li><li>But no ST segment elevation.  Can have EKG changes of ST depression or T wave inversion.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"11":"<ul><li>ST Depression: Non-ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction</li></ul>",
"12":"<ul><li>Acute Coronary Syndromes</li><li>ST-segment Elevation Myocardial Infarction (STEMI): Previously known as transmural infarction or Q-wave myocardial infarction<ul><li>STEMI is due to a COMPLETE coronary artery occlusion</li><li>Characterized by chest pain occurring at rest, with</li><li>ST segment elevation on a 12-lead EKG and</li><li>POSITIVE HS troponin</li></ul></li></ul>",
"13":"<ul><li>ST Segment Elevation:  Anterolateral STEMI</li><li>Notice the RECIPROCAL changes!</li></ul>",
"14":"<ul><li>Localizing the MI</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Location</td><td>ST changes</td><td>Reciprocal Changes</td><td>Coronary Artery</td></tr><tr><td>Anterior MI</td><td>V1-V4</td><td>None- 15 lead</td><td>LAD</td></tr><tr><td>Septal MI</td><td>V1-V2</td><td>None</td><td>LAD</td></tr><tr><td>Lateral MI</td><td>I, aVL, V5 and V6</td><td>II, III, aVF (inferior leads)</td><td>LCx</td></tr><tr><td>Inferior MI</td><td>II, III, aVF</td><td>I, aVL (lateral leads)</td><td>RCA (80%) or LCx (20%)</td></tr><tr><td>Posterior MI</td><td>V7-V9</td><td>V1-V3</td><td>RCA or LCx</td></tr><tr><td>RV MI</td><td>V1-V4 right sided EKG</td><td>I, aVL (right sided EKG)</td><td>RCA</td></tr></table>",
"15":"",
"16":"<ul><li>Unstable Don</li><li>Don is a 55-year-old who has lived hard.  He had chest pain 3 years ago and had a 60% lesion of the RCA evident on a cath - but was treated medically with metoprolol, ASA and has done well.</li><li>Today Don has the sudden onset of heavy substernal chest pain, dyspnea, diaphoresis and nausea while at work.</li><li>He’s transported to the hospital, receiving aspirin 325 mg x 1 and SL nitroglycerin on the way.</li></ul>",
"17":"<ul><li>Initial Therapeutics for Suspected ACS</li><li>Initiation of immediate therapy for patients with chest pain due to suspected ACS, often done by EMS or before any work-up is performed.</li><li>Aspirin 325 mg x1 is given-chewed/ swallowed to all possible and definite ACS patients.</li><li>Nitroglycerin SL or IV may be used in patients having active chest pain with a few exceptions-<ul><li>RV infarct, severe AS or use of PDE-5 inhibitors within 24hrs for erectile dysfxn or pulmonary hypertension.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"18":"<ul><li>Initial Therapeutics for Suspected ACS</li><li>Patients arrive in the ER and are assessed.</li><li>Patients who continue to have chest pain after treatment with 1-2 sublingual nitro are begun on a nitroglycerin drip.<ul><li>Contraindicated if hypotensive, or RV infarction</li></ul></li><li>Patients who continue to have chest pain despite nitrates or patients with STEMI are started on beta blockers.<ul><li>CI if HF, risk of cardiogenic shock, bradyarrhythmia</li></ul></li></ul>",
"19":"<ul><li>Drugs to Avoid for Suspected ACS</li><li>Morphine: try to avoid unless the patient is in severe pain, morphine may interfere with antiplatelet meds.</li><li>Oxygen: only use in patients with sats &lt; 90% due to lack of clinical benefit, there is also the potential risk of hyperoxemia induced coronary artery vasospasm.</li></ul>",
"20":"<ul><li>ACS Diagnostics</li><li>12 lead EKG - The first EKG may be non-diagnostic. Serial EKGs should be obtained (perform a repeat 20-30 minutes or sooner)</li><li>The usual labs - CBC, chemistry panel, PTT, PT/INR</li><li>BNP is often done to assess for heart failure.</li><li>CXR – evaluates for heart failure or other cause of potential chest pain.</li></ul>",
"21":"<ul><li>ACS Diagnostics</li><li>HS Troponin I<ul><li>Specific to cardiac muscle but remember that a high sensitivity assay will detect troponin in more than half of asymptomatic individuals.</li><li>Obtain a troponin at the time of arrival and again in 1-2 hours.</li><li>May be normal or minimally elevated if a patient presents quickly - but what is the delta (change) that occurs between the 1st and 2nd levels?</li><li>Troponin is chronically elevated in late-stage CKD, ESRD and any heart stress may cause leak.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"22":"<ul><li>Unstable Don</li><li>On exam at the time of ER arrival, Don is sweaty, breathing hard and pale, his chest pain has improved from a 12 to a 5/10 with pre-hospital care.</li><li>On exam, vitals are 98.3/24/112 and 170/92.  Lungs are clear, heart is tachycardic, no JVD</li><li>Don has received aspirin 325 mg x 1, SL nitro x 2, but still has pain.  A nitroglycerin gtt is hung.</li><li>A 12 lead EKG, CXR, chemistry panel, CBC, BNP, PT/INR, PTT and HS troponin I are ordered.</li></ul>",
"23":"<ul><li>Management of Unstable Angina/NSTEMI</li><li>Steps of therapy:<ul><li>Anti-anginal therapy<ul><li>Nitroglycerin (SL, IV): 0.4mg SL may be given q5 min x3 or a nitroglycerin gtt may be started at 5-10 mcg/min and titrated to relief of symptoms or drop in BP (keep systolic &gt; 100 mmHg)</li><li>Beta-blockers: an oral cardioselective BB is begun within 24 hours as long as the patient does not have decompensated HF or hypotension/bradycardia.  IV BB use is limited.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul>",
"24":"<ul><li>Management of Unstable Angina/NSTEMI<ul><li>Dual Anti-Platelet Therapy (DAPT)<ul><li>Uncoated aspirin 325 mg –chewed or crushed as the initial dose in the ER, then EC ASA 81 mg daily</li><li>Thienopyridine or P2Y12 inhibitor- for patients going to immediate LHC, decided with/by cardiology.<ul><li>Prasugrel (Effient)</li><li>Ticagrelor (Brilinta)</li><li>Clopidogrel (Plavix)</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></li></ul>",
"25":"<ul><li>Management of Unstable Angina/NSTEMI<ul><li>Anti-thrombotic therapy:<ul><li>Unfractionated Heparin (UFH)- given as an IV drip titrated to anti-Xa level or PTT if cath planned.</li><li>LMWH -Enoxaparin (Lovenox) given SQ 1 mg/kg twice daily if no cath is planned.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>These drugs inhibit clot extension by preventing further thrombin activation of platelets and coagulation factors.</li></ul>",
"26":"<ul><li>Management of Unstable Angina/NSTEMI<ul><li>Risk Stratify</li><li>Patients with NSTEMI or UA and one or more of the following features are unstable and should proceed to emergent cardiac catheterization.<ul><li>Cardiogenic shock</li><li>Severe LV dysfunction or florid heart failure</li><li>Recurrent/persistent rest angina despite intensive medical therapy with nitro and BB</li><li>New or worsening mitral regurgitation or new VSD</li><li>Sustained ventricular arrhythmias</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul>",
"27":"<ul><li>Management of Unstable Angina/NSTEMI</li><li>Risk Stratify<ul><li>In stable patients, a TIMI score is calculated to determine if the patient has high risk features, and these pts should proceed to LHC within 24-48 hours.</li><li>A TIMI score of 3+ is at increased risk:<ul><li>Age &gt;=65 (1 point)</li><li>3 or more CAD risk factors (1 point)</li><li>Known CAD with &gt; 50% stenosis (1 point)</li><li>Aspirin use in the past 7 days (1 point)</li><li>Severe angina in the preceding 24 hours (1 point)</li><li>Elevated cardiac markers (1 point)</li><li>ST deviation greater than 0.5 mm (1 point)</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul>",
"28":"<table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td></td><td>GRACE Risk Score (2.0)6,7</td><td>TIMI Risk Score for Unstable Angina/NSTEMI*4</td><td>TIMI Risk Score for STEMI5</td></tr><tr><td>Target population</td><td>ACS</td><td>Unstable angina or NSTEMI</td><td>STEMI</td></tr><tr><td>Target outcome(s)</td><td>In-hospital, 6-mo, 1-y, 3-y, death or death/MI</td><td>14-d all-cause death, MI, or urgent revascularization</td><td>30-d all-cause death</td></tr><tr><td>Variables used</td><td>In-hospital risk score\u000bAge\u000bKillip class\u000bSystolic blood pressure\u000bHeart rate\u000bST-segment deviation\u000bCardiac arrest on admission\u000bSerum creatinine\u000bElevated cardiac biomarkers</td><td>Age ≥65 y\u000b≥3 risk factors for CAD\u000bKnown coronary stenosis (≥50%)\u000bST-segment deviation ≥0.5 mm\u000b≥2 anginal events in prior 24 h\u000bAspirin use in prior 7 d\u000bElevated cardiac biomarkers (CK-MB or troponin)</td><td>Age (65-74 y/≥75 y) (2-3 points)\u000bKillip class II-IV (2 points)*\u000bSystolic blood pressure (&lt;100 mm Hg, 3 points)\u000bHeart rate &gt;100 bpm (2 points)\u000bAnterior ST-segment elevation or LBBB (1 point)\u000bDiabetes/hypertension/angina (1 point)\u000bWeight (&lt;67 kg, 1 point)\u000bTime to treatment &gt;4 h (1 point)</td></tr></table>",
"29":"<ul><li>Management of Unstable Angina/NSTEMI</li><li>After risk stratification, determine the invasive management strategy:<ul><li>1. Immediate Invasive – Very high-risk patients<ul><li>Immediate coronary angiography (emergent cath).</li><li>Examples: cardiogenic shock, hemodynamic instability, refractory chest pain despite medical therapy, or life-threatening arrhythmias.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul>",
"30":"<ul><li>Management of Unstable Angina/NSTEMI</li><li>2. Routine invasive – intermediate and high-risk patients<ul><li>Plan coronary angiography during the hospitalization, even if symptoms improve with medical therapy.</li><li>Timing depends on risk:<ul><li>Early: generally within 24 hours for selected high-risk patients.</li><li>Other patients: later during hospitalization, based on clinical risk and stability.</li></ul></li><li>PCI or CABG may be performed depending on coronary anatomy.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"31":"<ul><li>Management of Unstable Angina/NSTEMI</li><li>3. Selective invasive – Selected low-risk patients<ul><li>Begin with medical management and further risk assessment.</li><li>Perform stress testing or coronary CT angiography when appropriate.</li><li>Proceed to coronary angiography if testing demonstrates significant ischemia or symptoms recur.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"32":"<ul><li>Sunil V. Rao. Circulation. 2025 ACC/AHA/ACEP/NAEMSP/SCAI Guideline for the Management of Patients With Acute Coronary Syndromes: A Report of the American College of Cardiology/American Heart Association Joint Committee on Clinical Practice Guidelines, Volume: 151, Issue: 13, Pages: e771-e862, DOI: (10.1161/CIR.0000000000001309)</li><li>© 2025 by the American College of Cardiology Foundation and the American Heart Association, Inc.</li></ul>",
"33":"<ul><li>Management of Unstable Angina/NSTEMI</li><li>Contraindications for a Routine Invasive Approach in ACS:<ul><li>High risk for bleeding on DAPT<ul><li>Severe thrombocytopenia (platelet count &lt;50 × 109/L)<ul><li>Advanced kidney disease (not on dialysis)<ul><li>Acute renal failure<ul><li>Limited (&lt;1-2 y) life expectancy</li><li>Advanced dementia</li><li>Known coronary anatomy that precludes PCI and/or CABG surgery</li><li>Patient preference</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></li></ul>",
"34":"<ul><li>Management of Unstable Angina/NSTEMI</li><li>Medications in steps 1, 2 and 3 are initiated and cardiology is consulted from the ER in all cases of UA/NSTEMI.</li><li>In stable patients, electrolytes and hypoxia are corrected, fluid status is optimized</li><li>Stable patients are admitted to the telemetry floor, bedrest and kept npo at midnight.  Prior to this they can have a heart healthy diet.</li></ul>",
"35":"<ul><li>Unstable Don</li><li>Don’s EKG shows T wave inversion and ST segment depression in leads II, III and aVF.</li><li>His initial HS troponin is 234 (normal &lt; 15) and he has slight elevation of BNP.  Subsequent trops are 391, and 806 then 682.</li><li>What is the diagnosis?  What is Don’s TIMI score?  What approach should we take to treat him?<ul><li>Don, 55 yo, had a 60% RCA lesion on a cath 3 yrs ago- treated with daily ASA at baseline.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"36":"<ul><li>Management of STEMI</li><li>STEMI is a time-critical emergency that requires rapid restoration of coronary blood flow (reperfusion).</li><li>Most STEMIs result from acute coronary atherothrombosis, typically due to plaque rupture or erosion with thrombus formation. This often causes severe or complete coronary artery occlusion.</li></ul>",
"37":"<ul><li>Management of STEMI</li><li>Time is muscle! Prolonged ischemia causes progressive, irreversible myocardial injury. Necrosis generally begins in the subendocardium and spreads outward over time.</li><li>The goal is to restore coronary perfusion as quickly as possible to minimize infarct size and preserve left ventricular function.</li></ul>",
"38":"",
"39":"<ul><li>Management of STEMI</li><li>STEMI is hopefully identified prior to arrival to the hospital by the paramedic, who communicates with the ER attending and may be able to transmit the EKG from the field.</li><li>Patients may be brought briefly to the ER for stabilization and the same therapy steps as UA/ NSTEMI- but this is a short stay.</li><li>The priority is to get them to the cardiac cath lab.</li></ul>",
"40":"<ul><li>Management of STEMI</li><li>The goal of therapy is to OPEN THE CORONARY ARTERY ASAP to RESTORE PERFUSION!!!<ul><li>DOOR to BALLOON time goal is 90 minutes.</li><li>DOOR to NEEDLE time goal is 30 minutes, to administer thrombolytics if a PCI cannot be performed.</li></ul></li><li>Primary PCI is preferred when it can be performed within the recommended time frame. PCI generally provides better outcomes, including lower risk of reinfarction and intracranial hemorrhage.</li></ul>",
"41":"<ul><li>STEMI: Reperfusion Therapy</li><li>1. Primary PCI (preferred strategy)</li><li>Direct presentation to PCI-capable hospital: goal of first medical contact-to-device time ≤90 minutes.</li><li>Transfer from non-PCI hospital: goal of first medical contact-to-device time ≤120 minutes.</li></ul>",
"42":"<ul><li>STEMI: Reperfusion Therapy</li><li>2. Fibrinolytic therapy (when timely PCI is unavailable)</li><li>Consider if PCI cannot be performed within 120 minutes.</li><li>Patient must be eligible, generally within 12 hours of symptom onset.</li><li>Goal: door-to-needle time ≤30 minutes.</li><li>Transfer to a PCI-capable hospital after administration.</li></ul>",
"43":"<ul><li>Management of STEMI</li><li>An inter-hospital transfer is appropriate if you can get the patient to a PCI capable hospital’s cath lab within 120 minutes.</li><li>The use of thrombolytics is reserved for patients who cannot get to PCI within 2 hours and can be used up to 12 hours after symptom onset.</li><li>Emergent CABG has a substantial mortality risk and reserved for patients who fail PCI or lytics.</li></ul>",
"44":"<ul><li>STEMI: Reperfusion Timing Targets</li><li>The goal is to restore coronary blood flow as quickly as possible.</li><li>Door-to-ECG</li><li>≤ 10 minutes</li><li>Obtain a 12-lead ECG in patients with suspected ACS.</li><li>Rapid recognition helps activate the STEMI pathway.</li><li>Primary PCI</li><li>≤ 90 minutes</li><li>First medical contact to device activation for direct presentation to a PCI-capable hospital.</li><li>If transfer is required, target ≤ 120 minutes from first medical contact.</li><li>Fibrinolytic therapy</li><li>≤ 30 minutes</li><li>Door-to-needle target when timely primary PCI cannot be achieved.</li><li>For eligible STEMI patients, generally within 12 hours of symptom onset; assess contraindications.</li><li>After fibrinolysis, transfer the patient to a PCI-capable hospital for a pharmacoinvasive strategy.</li><li>Starting points differ: ECG = hospital arrival | PCI = first medical contact | Fibrinolysis = hospital arrival</li></ul>",
"45":"<ul><li>Management of STEMI</li><li>Initial intervention: Treat the culprit lesion</li><li>Perform urgent coronary angiography to identify the infarct-related (culprit) artery.</li><li>Perform primary PCI to restore blood flow through the culprit artery as quickly as possible.</li></ul>",
"46":"<ul><li>Management of STEMI</li><li>What if additional obstructive lesions are present?</li><li>In hemodynamically stable patients, complete revascularization is generally recommended when significant nonculprit lesions are present.</li><li>Additional lesions may be treated:<ul><li>During the initial PCI in selected patients, OR</li><li>During a staged PCI later during hospitalization or within 45 days.</li></ul></li><li>The decision depends on coronary anatomy, lesion complexity, renal function, and clinical stability.</li></ul>",
"47":"<ul><li>Management of STEMI</li><li>Important exception: Cardiogenic shock</li><li>Prioritize PCI of the culprit artery.</li><li>Do NOT routinely perform PCI of nonculprit arteries during the initial procedure because of increased risk of adverse outcomes.</li></ul>",
"48":"<ul><li>Management of STEMI</li><li>STEMI without cardiogenic shock + multivessel CAD = complete revascularization is generally recommended.</li><li>STEMI with cardiogenic shock = culprit-vessel PCI first; avoid routine multivessel PCI during the initial procedure.</li></ul>",
"49":"<ul><li>Management of STEMI</li><li>A patient presents with CP, elevated HS trop and ST elevation in V3 and V4.</li><li>Where is the culprit lesion?</li><li>What if the patient also has an 80% stenosis of the mid RCA?</li></ul>",
"50":"<ul><li>Unstable Don</li><li>How would the management be different if Don’s EKG shows T wave inversion and ST segment elevation in leads II, III and aVF with HS troponin I of 234 (normal &lt; 15) and a slight elevation of BNP?</li><li>How would management be different if Don were a resident of Minnesota at a small community hospital, 3 hours away from the nearest PCI capable hospital, when he developed these symptoms?</li></ul>",
"51":"<ul><li>MINOCA: Myocardial Infarction with Non-Obstructive Coronary Arteries</li><li>Key characteristics:</li><li>Accounts for approximately 5–15% of acute MI presentations, depending on the population studied.</li><li>More common in women and relatively younger patients.</li><li>Defined by no coronary stenosis ≥50% in a major epicardial artery.</li><li>Can present as either STEMI or NSTEMI.</li><li>Initially a working diagnosis requiring further evaluation.</li></ul>",
"52":"<ul><li>MINOCA: Myocardial Infarction with Non-Obstructive Coronary Arteries</li><li>True ischemic MINOCA:</li><li>Coronary vasospasm</li><li>SCAD without obstructive disease</li><li>Coronary embolism or thrombosis</li><li>Coronary microvascular dysfunction</li><li>Plaque rupture or erosion with transient thrombosis or no significant residual obstruction</li></ul>",
"53":"<ul><li>MINOCA: Myocardial Infarction with Non-Obstructive Coronary Arteries</li><li>MINOCA mimics:</li><li>Myocarditis</li><li>Takotsubo (stress-induced cardiomyopathy)</li><li>Pulmonary embolism</li><li>Other causes of myocardial injury</li></ul>",
"54":"<ul><li>Evaluation of Suspected Acute MI: Consider MINOCA</li><li>Suspected acute MI(Ischemic symptoms + ECG/troponin findings)</li><li>Coronary angiography</li><li>Obstructive CAD(≥50% stenosis)</li><li>No obstructive CAD(&lt;50% stenosis)</li><li>Evaluate coronary anatomyand manage according to the findings</li><li>Working diagnosis: MINOCA(if MI criteria are met)</li><li>Further evaluation, as appropriate:Cardiac MRI • Echocardiography • Intracoronary imagingVasospasm / microvascular testing</li><li>Identify the cause → tailor treatment</li><li>Important: MINOCA is a working diagnosis—not a final diagnosis. Exclude mimics such as myocarditis and Takotsubo syndrome.</li><li>MI requires acute myocardial injury plus evidence of acute myocardial ischemia.</li></ul>",
"55":"<ul><li>Takotsubo Cardiomyopathy (Stress-Induced Cardiomyopathy)</li><li>Causes transient regional left ventricular systolic dysfunction.</li><li>Can mimic ACS with chest pain, EKG changes, and elevated troponin.</li><li>Often associated with emotional or physical stress, although a trigger is not always identified.</li></ul>",
"56":"<ul><li>Vasospastic AnginaAlso called variant angina or Prinzmetal angina</li><li>Patients develop atypical chest pain usually occurring at rest, most commonly from midnight to morning.</li><li>Patients are typically young smokers with few cardiovascular risk factors.  History of cocaine or meth use is an important question to ask.</li><li>EKG recordings show a transient ST segment elevation which resolves after the pain subsides.</li></ul>",
"57":"<ul><li>Vasospastic AnginaAlso called variant angina or Prinzmetal angina</li><li>PANCE pearl:</li><li>Vasospastic angina is caused by coronary artery spasm rather than a fixed obstructive lesion. A patient can have transient ST elevation during an episode despite having no significant obstructive CAD on coronary angiography.</li></ul>",
"58":"<ul><li>Treatment:</li><li>First-line: Calcium channel blockers (e.g., amlodipine, diltiazem, verapamil).</li><li>For persistent symptoms: Long-acting nitrates may be added.</li><li>Acute episodes: Sublingual nitroglycerin.</li><li>Avoid smoking and precipitating substances, particularly cocaine and methamphetamine.</li><li>Beta-blockers, particularly nonselective agents, may worsen coronary vasospasm and are generally avoided when treating isolated vasospastic angina.</li><li>Vasospastic AnginaAlso called variant angina or Prinzmetal angina</li></ul>",
"59":"<ul><li>Stimulant-Associated Cardiac Ischemia</li><li>Cocaine and methamphetamine increase sympathetic activity, causing tachycardia, HTN, and increased myocardial oxygen demand.</li><li>Can cause coronary vasospasm, coronary thrombosis, and acute MI.</li><li>Ask about recent cocaine or methamphetamine use in patients presenting with chest pain.</li><li>Acute management:</li><li>Benzodiazepines to reduce sympathetic stimulation.</li><li>Nitrates for coronary vasospasm.</li><li>Calcium channel blockers may be used when appropriate.</li></ul>",
"60":"<ul><li>Right Ventricular Infarction</li><li>Usually caused by occlusion of the proximal right coronary artery (RCA) or one of its branches.</li><li>Commonly occurs in association with an inferior MI.</li><li>Classic clinical triad:<ul><li>Hypotension – decreased cardiac output.</li><li>JVD – increased right-sided filling pressures.</li><li>Clear lungs – typically no pulmonary edema in isolated RV infarction.</li></ul></li><li>RV dysfunction → decreased blood flow to lungs → decreased LV preload → decreased cardiac output → hypotension.</li></ul>",
"61":"<ul><li>Right Ventricular Infarction</li><li>Remember that the systemic blood pressure is dependent upon preload (blood being delivered to left atria/ventricle).</li><li>If right side fails, preload falls and hypotension results.</li><li>A right ventricular infarction is diagnosed by finding ST-segment elevation in right precordial leads on a right sided 12-lead.<ul><li>OBTAIN a RIGHT SIDED EKG on all patients with an INFERIOR MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION</li></ul></li></ul>",
"62":"<ul><li>Right Ventricular Infarction</li><li>Standard 12-lead ECG:</li><li>Uses six precordial (chest) leads, V1–V6.</li><li>Primarily evaluates the anterior, septal, and lateral portions of the left ventricle, along with other cardiac electrical views.</li><li>May identify an inferior MI but can miss right ventricular involvement.</li><li>Right-sided ECG:</li><li>Additional ECG leads are placed on the right side of the chest.</li><li>The most important lead is V4R, which directly evaluates the right ventricular free wall.</li><li>Helps identify right ventricular infarction, particularly when an inferior STEMI is present.</li></ul>",
"63":"<ul><li>Standard 12-lead ECG vs. right-sided ECG</li></ul>",
"64":"<ul><li>Right Sided EKG</li></ul>",
"65":"<ul><li>Right Ventricular Infarction</li><li>In a patient who has a right ventricular infarction the management has some key differences.</li><li>Avoid vasodilators (nitrolglycerin) and diuretics, as these drugs will worsen hypotension.</li><li>To treat hypotension, we will need to expand preload, so give volume- normal saline boluses IV.</li></ul>",
"66":"<ul><li>Recognize RV Infarction</li><li>Inferior STEMI + hypotension + JVD + clear lungs = suspect RV infarction.<ul><li>Obtain a right-sided ECG, particularly lead V4R.</li><li>Avoid nitrates and diuretics because they decrease preload.</li><li>Consider cautious IV fluid administration if hypotensive and preload deficient, with frequent reassessment.</li><li>Urgent reperfusion (usually PCI) is essential.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"67":"<ul><li>CAD in the Elderly</li><li>1. Higher prevalence and mortality<ul><li>Approximately 60–65% of MIs occur in patients ≥65 years.</li><li>Older adults account for the majority of ACS-related deaths.</li></ul></li><li>2. Atypical clinical presentation</li><li>More likely to present with NSTEMI than STEMI.</li><li>May present without classic chest pain.</li><li>Possible symptoms include:<ul><li>Dyspnea</li><li>Syncope</li><li>Unexplained weakness or fatigue</li><li>Acute confusion (delirium)</li></ul></li><li>Atypical presentations may delay recognition and treatment.</li><li>3. Increased risk of complications</li><li>Greater risk of acute heart failure and cardiogenic shock.</li><li>Increased risk of arrhythmias, bleeding, and acute kidney injury.</li><li>Higher overall mortality following MI.</li></ul>",
"68":"<table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Complications of AMI</td></tr><tr><td>FUNCTIONAL</td></tr><tr><td>Left ventricular failure (CHF)    Angiotensin Converting Enzyme Inhibitor</td></tr><tr><td>Right Ventricular Failure   GIVE FLUID, AVOID NITRO/DIURETIC</td></tr><tr><td>Cardiogenic Shock</td></tr><tr><td>MECHANICAL</td></tr><tr><td>Free-wall rupture  HIGH MORTALITY, complete heart block/ hemopericardium</td></tr><tr><td>Ventricular Septal Defect  harsh holosystolic murmur, “oxygen-step up”</td></tr><tr><td>Papillary muscle rupture with acute mitral regurgitation  pulmonary edema</td></tr><tr><td>ELECTRICAL</td></tr><tr><td>Bradyarrhythmias/ 2nd degree/3rd degree   Pacemaker</td></tr><tr><td>Tachyarrhythmias (Supraventricular Tachycardia, Ventricular Tachycardia)   may need anti-arrhythmics (amiodarone) or cardioversion if persistent</td></tr><tr><td>Bundle branch block/Fascicular blocks  may need pacemaker</td></tr></table>",
"69":"<ul><li>AMI Complications: Functional</li><li>Left ventricular failure: Pulmonary congestion and edema; treat with diuretics when congested and guideline-directed HF therapy (BB, ACEi)</li><li>Right ventricular failure: Hypotension with elevated JVP and clear lungs; cautious IV fluids if preload-dependent; avoid nitrates and diuretics when hypotensive</li><li>Cardiogenic shock: Severe pump failure and tissue hypoperfusion; urgent revascularization is essential</li></ul>",
"70":"<ul><li>AMI Complications: Mechanical</li><li>LV free-wall rupture: Hemopericardium → cardiac tamponade → PEA or sudden death</li><li>Ventricular septal rupture: New harsh holosystolic murmur; left-to-right shunt; oxygen saturation step-up</li><li>Papillary muscle rupture: Acute severe mitral regurgitation → pulmonary edema and cardiogenic shock</li></ul>",
"71":"<ul><li>AMI Complications: Electrical</li><li>BRADYARRHYTHMIAS / HEART BLOCK</li><li>Sinus bradycardia and AV block may occur, particularly with inferior MI</li><li>Atropine may be used for symptomatic bradycardia</li><li>Temporary pacing for refractory symptomatic or hemodynamically significant bradycardia</li><li>Permanent pacemaker only for persistent, clinically significant conduction disease after an appropriate waiting period</li><li>TACHYARRHYTHMIAS</li><li>VT/VF: Potentially life-threatening; defibrillation for pulseless VT/VF; synchronized cardioversion for unstable VT with a pulse</li><li>SVT: Treat according to rhythm and hemodynamic stability</li><li>CONDUCTION ABNORMALITIES</li><li>New bundle branch or fascicular block may indicate extensive myocardial injury</li><li>Isolated new bundle branch block does not automatically require a pacemaker</li></ul>",
"72":"<ul><li>Unstable Don</li><li>Unstable Don undergoes an emergent cardiac cath at Kettering Medical Center and is found to have a 99% occlusion of his mid-LAD and an 80% stenosis of his RCA.</li><li>A DES is placed in the LAD and Don is transferred to the CCU.</li></ul>",
"73":"<ul><li>Unstable Don</li><li>Which approach is appropriate for managing Don's remaining RCA disease?</li><li>A. No further treatment is necessary because the culprit lesion has been treated.</li><li>B. RCA PCI must be performed during the same procedure.</li><li>C. RCA PCI may be performed during the initial procedure or as a staged intervention, depending on clinical and procedural factors.</li><li>D. RCA PCI is contraindicated following STEMI.</li></ul>",
"74":"<ul><li>Unstable Don</li><li>How will the cardiologist take care of Don post- MI?</li><li>What medication will he go home on if his EF was 55%?</li></ul>",
"75":"<ul><li>Unstable Don</li><li>Immediately following PCI:</li><li>Continuous cardiac monitoring/telemetry.</li><li>Monitor vital signs and assess for recurrent ischemia or arrhythmias.</li><li>Assess access site for bleeding or hematoma.</li><li>Assess distal circulation (pulses, color, temperature, sensation).</li><li>Monitor renal function following contrast administration.</li><li>Follow access-specific instructions for activity and ambulation.</li></ul>",
"76":"<ul><li>Unstable Don</li><li>Discharge planning:</li><li>Selected uncomplicated STEMI patients may be discharged within 48–72 hours if clinically stable.</li><li>Establish a plan for any remaining coronary disease/revascularization.</li></ul>",
"77":"<ul><li>Cardiac Rehabilitation</li><li>Cardiac rehab is a medically supervised program to improve CV health in a patient who has had an MI, PTCA with stent, CABG, valve surgery, or HFrEF.</li><li>Participating in cardiac rehab has well-proven benefits including reducing cardiac and all-cause mortality, reducing morbidity and improving quality of life for cardiac patients.</li><li>Despite this, fewer than 30% of eligible patients participate in cardiac rehab in the US.</li></ul>",
"78":"<ul><li>Cardiac Rehabilitation</li><li>Cardiac rehab has three equally important parts:<ul><li>Exercise counseling and training</li><li>Counseling to reduce stress</li><li>Education on heart healthy living</li></ul></li><li>Patients are normally asked to attend 36 sessions over a three-month period, where they partake in supervised exercise sessions and undergo nutritional counseling and receive advice on lipids, diabetes, blood pressure, smoking cessation, and receive psychological support.</li></ul>",
"79":"<ul><li>Barriers to Cardiac Rehabilitation and Solutions</li><li>Cost/coverage: Coverage and out-of-pocket costs vary by insurance plan.</li><li>Access: Transportation, distance, scheduling, and availability can limit participation.</li><li>Patient factors: Lack of awareness, competing responsibilities, and difficulty making lifestyle changes can reduce participation.</li></ul>",
"80":"<ul><li>ACS: Core Secondary Prevention</li><li>Lifestyle modification and patient education<ul><li>Heart-healthy diet, physical activity, medication adherence</li></ul></li><li>Antiplatelet therapy<ul><li>Aspirin + P2Y12 inhibitor (DAPT), generally for 12 months after ACS if bleeding risk is not high</li></ul></li><li>High-intensity statin therapy<ul><li>Add nonstatin therapy when needed to achieve further LDL-C reduction</li></ul></li><li>Beta-blocker therapy<ul><li>Particularly important with reduced LVEF, heart failure, angina, or arrhythmias</li></ul></li><li>ACE inhibitor or ARB<ul><li>Especially with LVEF ≤40%, hypertension, diabetes, or anterior STEMI</li></ul></li></ul>",
"81":"<ul><li>ACS: Additional Secondary Prevention Measures</li><li>Lipid management: High-intensity statin; add ezetimibe or other nonstatin therapy as needed.</li><li>Cardiac rehabilitation: Referral for supervised exercise and comprehensive risk-factor modification.</li><li>Smoking cessation: Counseling and pharmacologic support when appropriate.</li><li>Weight management and nutrition: Encourage a heart-healthy diet and healthy weight.</li><li>Blood pressure control: Treat hypertension to an individualized goal.</li><li>Diabetes management: Individualize A1c goal; consider GLP-1 receptor agonist or SGLT2 inhibitor with cardiovascular benefit in patients with type 2 diabetes and ASCVD.</li><li>Treat heart failure when present: Guideline-directed medical therapy; diuretics for congestion.</li><li>Anticoagulation when indicated: For atrial fibrillation or LV thrombus—not routinely after every MI.</li></ul>"};
